Package: BiSCaL
Type: Package
Title: Bivariate Separable Causal Effects in Longitudinal Studies
Version: 0.1.0
Authors@R:
    person("Pingbo", "Hu", , "pingbo.hu@gmail.com", role = c("aut", "cre", "cph"))
Description: Estimates and tests separable causal treatment effects on two
    ordinal (including binary) responses that are measured repeatedly in a
    longitudinal study and may be subject to intermittent missingness and
    right-censoring. The treatment is conceptualized as having two
    components, each operating on one response. The total treatment effect at
    the end of follow-up, defined as the log ratio of generalized odds ratios
    of the two responses, then decomposes into separable treatment effects on
    each response whose sum equals twice the total effect. Under conditional
    exchangeability, consistency, positivity and conditions on the treatment
    components, the effects are identified by a recursive formula in the
    transition probabilities of the responses, which are modeled by
    baseline-category logistic regressions and estimated by maximum
    likelihood. The package estimates the effects for given covariate values
    and averaged over a covariate distribution, provides delta-method standard
    errors and Wald tests comparing the absolute separable effects on the two
    responses, and simulates data from the designs studied in Hu and Yi
    (2026) <doi:10.1002/sim.70320>.
